   "id": "cce32f6833d0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# GitHub Open-Source Repository Recommendation System\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nI built this project to answer a practical question: **can a developer's public GitHub history find relevant open-source repositories that popularity alone would miss?** The executed sample contains 5 public developers, 796 real repositories, and 449 owned/starred interactions from the GitHub REST API. I compare random, popularity, language-only, TFâ€“IDF content, and multi-signal hybrid rankers. Profiles use training history only, and all 5 developers are evaluated against their five most recent held-out stars.\n\n**What happened:** the simple `language` model ranked first, with test NDCG@10 **0.1357**, Hit Rate@10 **0.40**, Recall@10 **0.08**, and sampled-catalog coverage **7.01%**. Content reached 0.0476, compared with random 0.0000, popularity 0.0678, and language-only 0.1357. I keep that negative result because it is the useful result: in this small sample, primary language carried more reliable signal than the richer models. The main limitation is sample sizeâ€”five selected users demonstrate the engineering workflow, not population-level performance.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9de8cdfb1e7c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Business Problem\n",
    "\n",
    "GitHub makes popularity visible, but popularity is not the same as personal relevance. I want to help a developer find an unseen project that fits their languages and technical interests while still being active enough to explore or contribute to. The question I test is whether public activity and repository metadata can beat three simple alternatives: random choice, global popularity, and primary-language matching.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Target user:** a developer exploring or contributing to public open source.\n",
    "- **Unit of analysis:** a developerâ€“repository candidate pair.\n",
    "- **Decision supported:** which unseen public repositories to place in the top recommendation positions.\n",
    "- **Analytical question:** how accurately can held-out public interactions be retrieved in the top K?\n",
    "- **Success criteria:** rank-aware gains over baselines, useful catalog coverage/diversity, reproducibility, and explanations grounded in calculated features.\n",
    "- **Constraints:** public data only, API limits, incomplete optional metadata, and no claim of ranking all GitHub.\n",
    "- **Ethics/privacy:** only public professional activity is used; no private activity, sensitive-trait inference, or automated contribution decisions.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b014e75778b8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Project Objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "My objective is to produce an auditable ranking, not a black-box demo. Given one of the sampled usernames, the system removes repositories that person already owns or starred, ranks the remaining catalog, shows every score component, and explains why each item appeared. I then compare that ranking with simple baselines using held-out interactions that never enter the profile.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a21319cef263",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Data Sources\n",
    "\n",
    "The official GitHub REST API (version `2022-11-28`) supplies repository search results, public user profiles, and timestamped public stars. GitHub's public raw-content host supplies a bounded set of README and contribution-file checks. The first unauthenticated pass hit the hourly core limit, so I resumed from cache after reset instead of downloading the same responses again. All five committed histories ultimately came from the REST API. The collection manifest records the queries, sources, row counts, and failures.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ddbbf9fa6fd5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Data Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a five-person convenience sample, not a survey of GitHub. I keep at most the 100 most recent API-visible stars per developer and build the candidate catalog from selected language searches plus those histories. Byte-level language data and issue-label checks were too expensive for this unauthenticated run, so I leave those values missing instead of guessing. All five evaluation histories are chronological. A star is treated as a useful interest signal, but it does not prove contribution intent.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e5b87a8eb826",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
